    kwargs={"reasoning_content": "Duration=60000ms, pktSize=1024, UDP rate=100:\n- avg RTT = 2.729ms (much higher! up from 0.753)\n- max RTT = 4.1ms\n- loss_pct = 52.4%\n\nreward = 2.729 + 52.4*10 = 526.729\n\nInteresting! The RTT increased dramatically (0.75 → 2.73ms) with larger packets, but the loss_pct actually slightly decreased (53.5 → 52.4). \n\nSo reward = 526.7 vs previous 535.75. Slightly lower! Hmm.\n\nWait, the loss_pct went DOWN slightly despite longer duration. Why? Because with larger packets, maybe the target can process... no. Actually the true loss during attack: let me compute. During attack samples received ~4/10 each → ~60% loss. Over the 70s window with baseline at start (2 samples) and end (7 samples), fraction under attack = 60/70 = 86%. 60% * 86% = ~51%. Close to 52.4%.\n\nHmm, so with 1024B packets the true attack loss was ~60%, lower than the 64B case which had ~70%? That's odd. Maybe larger packets cause the target NIC to drop differently, or maybe the pktgen port reached its limit.\n\nActually, the key observation: with larger packets, RTT increased a lot (2.7ms) but loss stayed similar. reward = 2.73 + 524 = 526.7.\n\nCompare with 64B duration=30000: reward = 0.753 + 535 = 535.75.\n\nSo the 64B/30s was slightly better due to higher loss.\n\nHmm. So larger packets didn't help loss. Interesting — the RTT is higher but loss is what dominates reward.\n\nSo the strategy of maximizing loss is key. Longer duration helps. Let me reconsider:\n- 64B, 30s: loss 53.5\n- 1024B, 60s: loss 52.4\n\nThe 60s should have less dilution. But loss went down. So the true attack loss with 1024B is LOWER than with 64B. Odd.\n\nWait — maybe with 1024B packets, pktgen at rate=100 couldn't sustain the same packet rate? Actually pktgen rate=100 for larger packets means lower pps (since it's bandwidth-limited). Hmm, at 100G line rate, 1024B → ~12 Mpps, 64B → ~148 Mpps. So pps dropped by ~12x! That's why loss didn't increase — fewer packets per second.\n\nSo for maximum pps (packet-rate based DoS), smaller packets are better! Let me reconsider: 64B gives highest pps.\n\nSo the best approach for loss (packet-rate based): small packets + high rate + long duration.\n\nLet me reconsider: 64B at rate=100, duration=30000 gave loss 53.5%. Let me try 64B at rate=100 with even longer duration, OR try to combine.\n\nHmm wait, actually maybe I should reconsider whether more pps is possible. rate=100 is max. \n\nActually, wait: what about the multiplicity of pktgen ports? Pktgen has 4 ports. Maybe sending on multiple ports would increase throughput. But the tool uses port 0.\n\nHmm. Also, I could try count=0 (forever) — but that's dangerous.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The measured loss is limited by dilution (~10s overhead). Best so far: 64B, 30s → 53.5%. If I do 64B, 60s → dilution 60/70=86%, true loss ~70% → measured ~60%? But we saw with 1024B/60s the true loss was only 60%. With 64B the true loss was ~70%.\n\nLet me estimate 64B, 60s: measured loss ≈ 70% * 86% = 60%. reward ≈ 0.8 + 600 = 600. That'd be better.\n\nHmm, but iterations are almost up. I've done 9 iterations (1-9). Wait let me recount:\n1: scapy\n2: udp rate=10\n3: udp rate=50\n4: udp rate=80\n5: udp rate=100\n6: icmp rate=100\n7: udp rate=100 dur=15s\n8: udp rate=100 dur=30s\n9: udp rate=100 dur=60s pktSize=1024 (current)\n\nSo this is iteration 9. I have 1 iteration left (10).\n\nLet me use iteration 10 for the best config: 64B, longest duration, rate=100. That would be udp rate=100 duration=60000 pktSize=64.\n\nWait, but 60s takes a long time. Let me consider — the previous 64B/30s gave 53.5%. 64B/60s might give ~60%. Let me do it.\n\nActually, hmm, let me reconsider. Is the \"overhead\" really fixed at 10s? \n- 5s → 15s window (overhead 10s)\n- 15s → 25s (overhead 10s)\n- 30s → 40s (overhead 10s)\n- 60s → 70s (overhead 10s)\nYes, consistent 10s overhead.\n\nSo fraction under attack: 30/40=75% gave 53.5% → implied true loss 71%.\n60/70=86% with 1024B gave 52.4% → implied true loss 61% (lower due to fewer pps).\n\nFor 64B/60s: 86% * 71% = 61%. reward ≈ 0.8 + 610 = 611.\n\nThat's the best. Let me do that as iteration 10.\n\nBut wait — should I reset first? Yes, must reset before next pktgen. Let me reset and log iteration 9, then do iteration 10.\n\nLet me reset.", "refusal": null}
