     c.stop(ports=[0])\n    finally:\n        c.disconnect()\n```\n\nFor STLVmWrFlowVar to write an IP, need to add the size and also fix IPv4 checksum with STLVmFixIpv4. The WR flow var `pkt_offset=\"IP.src\"`. Good.\n\nNote: In TRex, when using `Ether()/IP()`, need to be careful that TRex STL parses via scapy. Actually TRex uses its own packet builder from scapy. This is standard.\n\nFor \"每个源IP出现多次\" — I'll set the FlowVar range to a smaller set and mention that with continuous mode, each IP is reused. Or better, I can implement it explicitly: use `op=\"inc\"` but set the range so the number of packets far exceeds number of IPs. Actually inc cycles through; each IP appears multiple times over the duration. That satisfies \"不能只出现一次\".\n\nBut to be more explicit about \"每个源IP必须有多个包\", I can design a VM where source IP increments every 3 packets. There's no direct \"every N packets\" op, but we can use two flow vars. Alternatively, simpler: I'll explain in comments that the source range is intentionally small (e.g., 50 IPs) while pps is high and duration long, so each source appears many times, defeating the first-packet-drop rule.\n\nHmm, actually there's a cleaner TRex mechanism: `STLVmFlowVar` with `op=\"inc\"` and `step`. Or we can use the multi-packet approach with a burst stream.\n\nFor SYN flood with retransmission, I can build a VM program sending SYN twice per flow... that's complex. Let me instead use two streams: one for initial SYN, one for retransmit SYN, both cycling the same source range so effectively each source sends 2 SYNs. Actually simpler: use `STLTXCont` with the range and note repetition.\n\nLet me think about \"首包丢弃规则\" more concretely. It's like: a firewall/IDS drops the first packet from any new source (to defeat single-packet spoofed floods). So an attacker whose flood sends each spoofed source only once gets fully dropped. To bypass, send 2+ packets per source. TRex STL: if we set the source IP range to N addresses and run at pps, each so