aiming to be gateway. Vary the claimed IP/MAC.\n\nVM write offsets for ARP:\nEther = 14 bytes.\nARP: hwtype(2)=0-1, ptype(2)=2-3, hwlen(1)=4, plen(1)=5, op(2)=6-7, hwsrc(6)=8-13, psrc(4)=14-17, hwdst(6)=18-23, pdst(4)=24-27.\nSo ARP starts at offset 14. So psrc at 14+14=28, hwsrc at 14+8=22, pdst at 14+24=38, etc.\n\nFor ARP poisoning we usually want to vary the source MAC / sender—but the simplest is to repeatedly send a fixed forged ARP reply (gateway IP mapped to attacker MAC). For a flood, vary the target IP (spoof many hosts).\n\nLet me implement ARP spoof: forge ARP reply: hwsrc=attacker MAC (or random), psrc=gateway IP (e.g., 10.0.0.1), hwdst=broadcast, pdst=victim IP range. Vary psrc over subnet to poison many entries.\n\nARP flooding: send ARP requests for random IPs to overwhelm neighbor table.\n\nVLAN hopping double tagging: Ether()/Dot1Q(vlan=outer)/Dot1Q(vlan=inner)/IP()/...\n\nVLAN brute force: vary Dot1Q vlan id 1..4094 with `op=\"inc\"` and size 2, min=1 max=4094, loop.\n\nSTP BPDU: use LLC/SNAP. Scapy has `Dot3()/LLC()/STP()` or `Ether()/LLC()`? Actually STP is 802.3 with length field. Scapy: `Dot3(dst=\"01:80:c2:00:00:00\", src=..., len=...) / LLC(dsap=0x42, ssap=0x42, ctrl=3) / STP(...)`. Let me craft it.\n\nScapy STP layer exists: `from scapy.layers.l2 import STP`. Fields: rootid, rootmac, bridgeid, bridgemac, portid, pathcost, etc.\n\nLet me build:\n```python\nbpdu = Dot3(src=..., dst=\"01:80:c2:00:00:00\") / LLC() / STP(rootid=0, rootmac=..., bridgeid=..., bridgemac=..., portid=1)\n```\nThe len field auto? Dot3 len is set by scapy automatically when building? Sometimes needs manual. Let me set `Dot3(..., len=...)`. Hmm.\n\nActually the STP attack: root bridge hijack, send BPDU with low bridge priority (0) to become root.\n\nDTP: DTP uses SNAP with ethertype 0x2004 (DTP). Scapy may not have DTP layer. We can craft raw bytes: `Ether(dst=\"01:00:0c:cc:cc:cc\", src=..., type=0x2004)/...`. Or use a raw packet with the DTP payload. Let me build with 