    kwargs={"reasoning_content": "Round 7 (jumbo):\n- TX_PPS = 1.47M, TX_BPS = 105.7 Gbps (!!! hit 100G line rate), CPU 58%, QFULL 8.16M.\n- BUT ping RTT = 0.064ms, loss = **0.0%**!\n\nSo jumbo frames reached 105.7Gbps at TRex TX but had ZERO effect on the target's ping. This confirms the hypothesis: **the path to 100.1.11.3 has a 1500 MTU** — jumbo frames are DROPPED at the switch (path MTU), never reaching the target, so the bottleneck link isn't saturated → ping unaffected. The 105.7Gbps is only on the TRex→first-switch hop, which isn't the bottleneck.\n\nGreat, this definitively tells me: **path MTU = 1500**, and the bottleneck (~25G) is DOWNSTREAM near the target. Jumbo is useless. Revert.\n\nThis also refines the model: the effective congestion is on the switch→target 25G link (MTU 1500). So I must send standard ≤1514B frames to reach it.\n\nSo the ceiling with 1514B frames: TRex max ~67Gbps (round5 static) into a ~25G bottleneck → best loss ~58-65%.\n\nNow, the best so far is round4 65.2%. To beat it, I need to either push more 1514B-frame bandwidth (beyond 67G — but that's the TRex/port limit apparently) OR use packets that individually cause the bottleneck to drop ICMP more.\n\nWait — reconsider. Round5 static reached 67Gbps (1514B) with QFULL. Why couldn't it reach higher? 67Gbps at 1514B = 5.5Mpps. The port nominally 100G but only did 67G TX for 1514B frames. Why? Maybe the port/PCIe can't do more than ~5.5Mpps TX regardless of frame size (PCIe/descriptor limit ~5.5M descriptors/s). At 9000B frames it did 1.47Mpps but 105Gbps (bandwidth-capped). So the limit is: min(PCIe bytes/s ≈ 105Gbps, PCIe descriptor/s ≈ 5.5Mpps). For 1514B: 5.5Mpps*1514*8=66.6Gbps (descriptor-limited). For 9000B: 105Gbps but capped by port ~100G... wait it did 105.7G which exceeds 100G?? Ethernet 100G nominal is 100e9 but with IFG overhead L1... 105.7e9 might be counting differently. Anyway.\n\nSo for 1514B frames, TRex capped at ~5.5Mpps = 66.6Gbps due to descriptor/pps limit. To get MORE bandwidth with 1514B I'd need >5.5Mpps which TRex can't.\n\nBUT — what if I use a frame size BETWEEN 1514 and 9000 that's still ≤1500 MTU? No, MTU 1500 caps at 1514. So max standard frame = 1514B.\n\nAlternatively: what if the path MTU is actually higher than 1500 but the switch drops jumbo only at some point... The 0% loss at 105G proves frames didn't reach the bottleneck link. So ≤1514 needed.\n\nTherefore, the best achievable is ~5.5Mpps @1514B = 66.6Gbps demand into 25G bottleneck → loss ~ (66.6-25)/66.6 = 62.5%. Round4 got 65.2% (with VM, 5.09Mpps, 61.8G but diverse). Round5 58.2%.\n\nHmm round4 65.2% exceeds the 62.5% theoretical for 61.8G — measurement variance / queue dynamics. \n\nTo maximize, I want: max pps at 1514B (→ ~5.5Mpps, 66.6G) AND flow diversity. Round4 was VM (diverse) but only 5.09Mpps. Round5 static 5.5Mpps but not diverse. \n\nBest combo: try to get ~5.5Mpps WITH some diversity. The VM cost limits pps. Maybe use a lighter VM: only vary srcip (1 flowvar) at 1514B — that was round4? No round4 was srcip+sport. Let me try srcip-ONLY at 1514B, pps high (15M), hoping ~5.3Mpps and diversity via srcip → maybe beat 65%.\n\nActually round3 was srcip+sport at 8M req → 4.0Mpps/48.8G/44.8%. Round4 srcip+sport 15M req → 5.09M/61.8G/65.2%. So higher pps request → higher achieved. Let me push pps request even higher (e.g., 30M) with srcip-only (lighter) at 1514B to try to reach 5.5Mpps. \n\nWait, achieved pps is limited by CPU per-packet, not by request. Requesting higher doesn't increase beyond CPU limit (round4 req 15M gave 5.09M; round5 static req 12M gave 5.5M). So CPU-limited. srcip-only VM has 3 instructions vs srcip+sport 5 instructions → fewer instructions → higher achievable pps. Let me try srcip-only at 1514B, request 30M, hope ~5.5Mpps with diversity.\n\nBut round5 static (0 VM) only got 5.5Mpps. srcip-only VM adds ~3 instructions → maybe 5.2Mpps. Close.\n\nHonestly, diminishing returns. The difference between 58-65% is measurement noise o